.TH EDI-AGENT-PACKAGING 7 "2026" "EDI Agent 1.7.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
edi-agent-packaging \- how EDI Agent is packaged, and how to package Python
projects like it
.SH DESCRIPTION
This page explains how
.B edi-agent
went from "a couple of scripts run by hand" to a properly packaged Python
project, as a worked example for future Python projects.
.SH THE PROBLEM BEING SOLVED
Before packaging was set up,
.B install.sh
worked by hand-writing a small bash wrapper at
.IR ~/.local/bin/edi-agent :
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/bash
"$PROJECT_DIR/venv/bin/python" "$PROJECT_DIR/edi_agent.py" "$@"
.fi
.RE
.PP
This works, but it's fragile: nothing declares what version is installed,
nothing declares the PySide6 dependency in a machine-readable way, and
there's no distinction between "the code" and "an installed thing you can
version, depend on, or eventually publish."
.SH THE FIX: pyproject.toml
Modern Python packaging is driven by a single
.I pyproject.toml
file at the project root. EDI Agent's looks like this (trimmed):
.RS
.nf
[build-system]
requires = ["setuptools>=68"]
build-backend = "setuptools.build_meta"

[project]
name = "edi-agent"
dynamic = ["version"]
dependencies = ["PySide6>=6.5.0"]

[project.scripts]
edi-agent = "edi_agent:main"

[tool.setuptools]
py-modules = ["edi_agent", "manual", "style"]

[tool.setuptools.dynamic]
version = { attr = "edi_agent.__version__" }
.fi
.RE
.PP
Three parts matter:
.TP
.B [project.scripts]
This is what actually creates the
.B edi-agent
command. `edi-agent = "edi_agent:main"` means: install a console script
named
.BR edi-agent ,
and have it call the
.B main()
function inside the
.B edi_agent
module. This is why
.I edi_agent.py
now has a real
.B main()
function instead of everything living under
.BR "if __name__ == \(dq__main__\(dq:" .
Any Python project that wants a CLI command needs exactly this shape:
a callable function, referenced here.
.TP
.B [tool.setuptools] py-modules
EDI Agent isn't a nested package (no
.I edi_agent/__init__.py
subdirectory) \(em it's three flat
.I .py
files at the repo root. `py-modules` tells setuptools to package those
three files directly, instead of looking for a package directory. If a
project already has an
.I import-name/
subdirectory with an
.IR __init__.py ,
use
.B packages
(or `packages = find:`) instead.
.TP
.B [tool.setuptools.dynamic] version
Rather than duplicating the version number in both
.I edi_agent.py
and
.IR pyproject.toml ,
this tells setuptools to read
.B __version__
directly out of the module. One source of truth, bumped in exactly one
place (still
.I edi_agent.py
itself).
.SH THE INSTALL: EDITABLE MODE
.B install.sh
runs:
.RS
.nf
pip install -e .
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-e
(editable) flag matters specifically for this project. EDI Agent loads its
logo and
.I manual.py
using paths relative to its own source file
.RI ( "Path(__file__).parent" ).
A normal
.RB "(non-editable) " "pip install"
copies the module into
.IR site-packages ,
which would separate it from
.I assets/
and break that path resolution unless the data files were also bundled and
the code updated to find them. An editable install instead leaves the code
running from this checked-out directory and just adds an
.B edi-agent
entry-point script to the venv's
.IR bin/ ,
pointing back at it. That keeps the "load sibling files by relative path"
design working with zero code changes.
.PP
The practical effect for
.B install.sh
is that instead of hand-writing the wrapper script above, it now does:
.RS
.nf
"$PROJECT_DIR/venv/bin/pip" install -e "$PROJECT_DIR"
ln -sf "$PROJECT_DIR/venv/bin/edi-agent" "$BIN_DIR/edi-agent"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B pip
generates the
.I venv/bin/edi-agent
script itself (from the
.B [project.scripts]
entry above); the install script just symlinks it onto
.IR PATH .
.SH TRYING IT YOURSELF
From a fresh clone, without running the full installer:
.RS
.nf
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
edi-agent list
.fi
.RE
.SH WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
This setup makes EDI Agent installable from source with a real entry point
and version metadata. It does
.B not
currently:
.IP \(bu 3
Publish to PyPI (would need a PyPI account, `python -m build`, and
`twine upload` \(em straightforward to add later, deliberately not done
since it requires credentials only the project owner can create).
.IP \(bu 3
Build a Fedora RPM or COPR package (a reasonable next step for a project
this targeted at Fedora specifically).
.IP \(bu 3
Bundle a Flatpak (heavier lift, matters more if wider desktop distribution
becomes a goal).
.PP
See
.I CLAUDE.md
in the repository root for the current state of these ideas.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR edi-agent (1),
the Python Packaging User Guide (packaging.python.org)
